+# Agent Note: Cordis Fabric API modules
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-08-06-cordis-fabric-api.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+Cordis plus [`cordis-fabric`](2026-08-06-cordis-fabric.md) supplies the DSH equivalent of a Fabric Loader with Mixin support: Cordis owns plugin discovery, dependency injection, and lifecycle, while `dsh-cordis-fabric` supplies pre-evaluation code transformation and runtime patch dispatch. It does not supply the equivalent of Minecraft's Fabric API.
+
+A plugin can already use DSH's domain-owned services and typed events, but those contracts follow product package boundaries and may expose details that a third-party Cordis Mod should not need to track. The low-level `ctx.fabric` patch service is not a substitute: its targets depend on package versions, emitted file paths, and AST shapes; it grants process-level code authority; and competing patches require conflict coordination. Treating that patch service as "Fabric API" would make the least stable and most privileged extension mechanism the default authoring surface.
+
+DSH therefore needs an optional, cooperative API library above Cordis and below Mods. It must curate stable extension contracts over existing authoritative services and events, preserve Cordis disposal and DSH logging/security invariants, and reserve Fabric patches for gaps where no cooperative seam exists.
+
+## Decision
+
+[`cordis-fabric-api`](../../../../packages/self-modification/cordis-fabric-api/README.md) at `packages/self-modification/cordis-fabric-api/` is the DSH counterpart of Minecraft's Fabric API: an optional library of modular Cordis plugins and compatibility facades. It does not load Mods, replace Cordis, or grant low-level Fabric patch authority. A Mod remains an ordinary Cordis plugin and declares injection of only the Fabric API module services it consumes.
+
+The architecture has three explicit layers:
+
+| Layer | Owner | Contract |
+|---|---|---|
+| Mod loader | Cordis Loader | Discovers configured plugins, resolves injection, mounts fibers, and disposes effects. |
+| Mixin subsystem | `cordis-fabric` | Transforms target code and dispatches trusted low-level patches. |
+| Cooperative Mod API | `cordis-fabric-api` | Exposes stable, domain-level registrations and events backed by existing DSH owners. |
+
+The API package delegates to existing services and events rather than creating a second event bus, tool registry, prompt store, or command registry. Each contribution is a Cordis effect, and each facade preserves the authoritative owner's ordering, cancellation, durability, and logging semantics.
+
+### Package topology
+
+One npm package with subpath plugin entries carries the modules; it is not split into one package per module, because versioning and installation stay simple while each entry retains an explicit injection list and platform closure.
+
+| Export | Cordis service | Platform | Delegates to |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `.` | Host bundle: mounts `ctx.fabricAgent`, `ctx.fabricTools`, `ctx.fabricPrompt`, and `ctx.fabricCommands` | Host | the four Host modules below |
+| `./agent` | `ctx.fabricAgent` | Host | `agent/*` events and `agent.inject()` |
+| `./tools` | `ctx.fabricTools` | Host | `ctx.tools` and `tools/*` |
+| `./prompt` | `ctx.fabricPrompt` | Host | `ctx.systemPrompt` |
+| `./commands` | `ctx.fabricCommands` | Host | the Host command registry |
+| `./compat` | `ctx.fabricCompat` | Host | low-level `dsh-cordis-fabric` patches (gap adapter) |
+| `./client` | `ctx.fabricClient` | Web | `@deepseek-ai/dsh-client-ui-command` and `@deepseek-ai/dsh-client-ui-slots` |
+| `./invariant` | — | Host | package-owned runtime relationship checks |
+
+The root entry is the standard Host bundle; every subpath is also directly mountable for thin compositions. The browser entry follows the `dshClient` package field and a disabled web-roster row: it is opt-in, builds as a normal client artifact, and imports no Node-only module. As a dual-face package it registers project references in both `tsconfig.host.json` and `tsconfig.client.json`, following the `cordis-fabric` precedent, and the package-layout rule and its gate allow this dual registration explicitly.
+
+No `ctx.fabricApi` service locator exists. Domain facades stay independently injectable, so missing topology is visible to Cordis and disposal ownership remains local. The package does not wrap every DSH service: a module entered Fabric API only when a real Mod consumer needed a compatibility boundary that the domain service itself does not promise — the fixture Mod exercises all five modules.
+
+### Module contracts
+
+**Agent API.** `ctx.fabricAgent` exposes a stable subset of agent lifecycle observation (`onCreated`, `onDisposed`, `onStatus`, `onSettled`) and operation-local context injection (`inject`). It never exposes the concrete `dsh-agent-loop`, private queue state, or mutable session internals. Callbacks receive the live Agent only where the owning event already does.
+
+**Tool API.** `ctx.fabricTools` registers tools and pre/post execution listeners through `ctx.tools`. It cannot bypass policy, approval, timeout, logging, cancellation, rendering, or the authoritative executor. A Fabric API tool has the same schema and result obligations as a native DSH tool, including model-visible logging and render intent. A facade listener on a Cordis waterfall calls `next()` unless it intentionally vetoes; returning without delegation is the documented veto.
+
+**Prompt API.** `ctx.fabricPrompt` registers ordered system sections, cache-safe contexts, tool-schema providers, and variables through `ctx.systemPrompt`. There is no shortcut that inserts unlogged model-visible text or assembles provider requests directly.
+
+**Command API.** `ctx.fabricCommands` registers human commands through the existing command registry. Commands remain outside model turns unless the owning command contract explicitly starts one. Registration conflicts, availability, and disposal use the domain registry's rules.
+
+**Client API.** `ctx.fabricClient` exposes named UI slots through `@deepseek-ai/dsh-client-ui-slots` and client commands through `@deepseek-ai/dsh-client-ui-command`. It does not expose raw DOM access, transport internals, or Host capabilities. The slot registration face is intentionally narrow (`FabricSlotOptions`): the full SlotMap type machinery lives in `dsh-client-ui-slots`, whose declaration merging only sees the packages each consumer imports.
+
+The first wave does not include generic networking, storage, themes/assets, or a universal registry. DSH already has domain services for settings, credentials, skills, storage, sessions, and providers; future Fabric API modules adapt them only when concrete Mod use cases require a narrower compatibility contract.
+
+### Patch-backed adapters
+
+A Fabric API module may use `cordis-fabric` internally when the target domain has no cooperative extension point and changing that domain owner is not appropriate. The public module contract remains an event or registry and never exposes `FabricPatch`, AST selectors, file paths, or `invoke()`.
+
+Every patch-backed adapter:
+
+- requires both the low-level `fabric` service and an explicit hook-installation capability/status from `dsh-cordis-fabric`; resolving `ctx.fabric` alone is insufficient because the service can exist without load-time hooks or the bridge being installed;
+- fails loud when that installation capability is absent instead of registering a patch that can never take effect;
+- pins module name, semver range, emitted file path, and selector in the adapter implementation;
+- owns patch registration as the module fiber's effect;
+- rejects ambiguous matches and exclusive replacement conflicts;
+- carries a keyless assembled test against the supported target version; and
+- documents that target version drift leaves the adapter unavailable rather than pretending compatibility.
+
+The `./compat` module ships the first adapter (`ctx.fabricCompat`): its targets are declared statically in the module config, `buildCompatInstrumentations` produces the load-time instrumentations (installed before the target module is imported), and `observe(name, listener)` registers the low-level patch as the calling fiber's effect and dispatches to the listeners for that target name. The public contract exposes only the declared target names and never `FabricPatch`, AST selectors, file paths, or `invoke()`. Cooperative domain changes remain preferred; a patch-backed adapter is a compatibility implementation detail, not permission for API consumers to depend on private code.
+
+### Compatibility, trust, and versioning
+
+The npm package version is the compatibility boundary; there is no second runtime semver protocol or Mod metadata format. Mods express compatibility through ordinary package dependencies and Cordis injection. Individual module contracts remain additive within a major version, and breaking event payload, ordering, or disposal changes require a package major version.
+
+The package starts at the repository's pre-release version and makes no stability claim beyond the first-wave compatibility suite. Stabilization freezes service keys, event ordering, error behavior, Host/Web availability, and the rule that registrations are effects. Existing DSH plugins need not migrate; Fabric API is a compatibility layer for Mods that choose it.
+
+Fabric API is safe to grant more broadly than `ctx.fabric`, but it is not automatically available to model-written temporary plugins. Each facade reaches real process capabilities through its owning service; repository and temporary-plugin policies grant modules explicitly, and the facade does not widen the authority of the service it delegates to.
+
+## Testing
+
+`tests/agent.spec.ts`, `tests/tools.spec.ts`, `tests/prompt.spec.ts`, and `tests/commands.spec.ts` cover each Host facade: delegation to the authoritative registry or event, disposer semantics, authoritative validation failures (duplicate names, invalid schema/variable names), the waterfall veto contract, and HMR-safety (fiber disposal removes the contribution).
+
+`tests/client.spec.ts` mounts the browser entry over the real `@deepseek-ai/dsh-client-ui-command` CommandService with fake slash/sessions/connection faces plus a fake slots registry: command contributions reach the authoritative client service (duplicate registration fails loud, disposal frees the name), slot registration delegates with its disposer, and a contributing fiber's disposal frees its command name (HMR safety).
+
+`tests/client-assembly.spec.ts` boots the real browser command and slot services (`SlotsService` + `CommandService`) with the real Loader loading the unmodified browser fixture Mod under `tests/fixtures/node_modules/fabric-client-fixture-mod/`: the command contribution occupies the authoritative registry (duplicate fails loud), the slot contribution occupies the single `root` hole and emits `slots/changed`, and `ctx.loader.remove(id)` frees both.
+
+`tests/assembly.spec.ts` boots a real Loader composition (SystemPrompt + ToolRegistry + CommandService + the Host bundle + Loader) that loads the unmodified fixture Mod under `tests/fixtures/node_modules/fabric-api-fixture-mod/`. The assembled app observes all four Mod effects through the authoritative services and the real event bus, and `ctx.loader.remove(id)` removes every contribution.
+
+`tests/compat.spec.ts` runs fresh child processes against the `fabric-compat-target` fixture: the compat instrumentation is installed before the target import, `observe` dispatches each call to its listeners and stops on the disposer, an absent bridge fails loud even though `ctx.fabric` resolves, and an unknown target name fails loud.
+
+## Consequences
+
+The facades are curated subsets, not complete mirrors: the module inventory selects a small stable subset, and the conformance tests exercise delegation rather than reimplement behavior. A wrapper that merely renames every domain method stays out of the package.
+
+The package cannot become a dumping ground: every module and operation requires a current Mod consumer and an authoritative domain owner; generic registries and convenience helpers stay out until that evidence exists.
+
+One npm package declares all dependencies, so dependency fan-in is a shared cost of the package even though subpath plugins and separate Host/client entries limit closure. If bundling evidence shows unavoidable cross-platform weight, the affected module splits before v1.
+
+Compatibility promises can freeze the wrong seams: the package remains pre-release until assembled Mods prove the first wave, and stabilization freezes only curated contracts, not every underlying DSH type.
+
+Patch-backed adapters would inherit source fragility: a supported package update can make an adapter stop matching. Version-pinned tests and fail-loud activation diagnostics reduce accidental use, but they cannot make a private target stable.
+
+A facade can bypass logging or policy accidentally: tool, prompt, command, and client modules delegate at the authoritative operation boundary, and the assembly tests verify that model-visible and privileged effects pass through their owners.
+
+## Deferred
+
+- **Catalog listing of the `fabricCompat` service.** The Cordis catalog projector covers the module services (the generator's service-location pattern includes their entry files), so `ctx.fabricAgent` and friends appear in the generated catalog; `fabricCompat` stays out by design — it is a config-driven adapter, not a module service, and is documented in the package README.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+**Call the existing DSH services and events "Fabric API" without a package.** They are already the preferred internal extension seams, but this provides no curated compatibility boundary, no Mod-oriented module matrix, and no place to stabilize adapters independently of product package organization.
+
+**Expose `ctx.fabric` as Fabric API.** Rejected because it makes source-shape coupling and process-level patch authority the default Mod contract. Low-level patches remain the Mixin subsystem and an escape hatch for API implementations.
+
+**Create one `ctx.fabricApi` mega-service.** Rejected because a service locator hides missing dependencies, combines unrelated domain ownership, and makes thin Host/Web compositions import capabilities they do not use. Independently injected module services keep topology explicit.
+
+**Create one npm package per module immediately.** Rejected until dependency closure or release cadence requires it. Subpath plugins provide modular mounting without committing the repository to a package family before consumers prove the boundaries.
+
+**Generate facades from the Cordis event/service catalog.** Rejected because type projection cannot choose which events are stable, preserve domain-specific ordering and authority, or decide which model-visible effects require logging. Fabric API contracts require deliberate curation.
+
+**Implement missing hooks only with Fabric patches.** Rejected as a default because version/file/AST coupling would leak into every module. The domain owner should expose a cooperative seam when that is a sound product contract; patches are reserved for compatibility gaps.
+
+## Related
+
+- [Cordis Fabric extension layer](2026-08-06-cordis-fabric.md) — the implemented Loader/Mixin subsystem this cooperative API sits above.
+- [DeepSeek Harness architecture](../../../../docs/architecture.md) — the authoritative service, event, capability, logging, and composition map the facades delegate to.
+- [`cordis-fabric` README](../../../../packages/self-modification/cordis-fabric/README.md) — the low-level patch API and its security/compatibility limitations.
