+# Agent Note: Cordis Fabric extension layer
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-08-06-cordis-fabric.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+Cordis gives plugins explicit services, events, dependency injection, and lifecycle ownership, but it does not transform a plugin module before the module is evaluated. A plugin that needs to change another plugin's private function, constructor, or first-import behavior therefore cannot rely on ordinary `ctx.on()`, `ctx.waterfall()`, or `ctx.intercept()`.
+
+Fabric solves the analogous Mod problem by transforming JVM bytecode before the target class is defined. DSH needs an opt-in equivalent for trusted Cordis extensions without changing the target plugin's source, while preserving Cordis disposal, Loader composition, Node/Web separation, and the repository's fail-loud and security boundaries.
+
+A direct monkey patch is insufficient as a foundation: it cannot reach module-private closures, is sensitive to load timing, and has no deterministic conflict or source-map contract. A Cordis-only event API is insufficient when the target plugin has no cooperative extension point.
+
+## Decision
+
+The [`cordis-fabric`](../../../../packages/self-modification/cordis-fabric/README.md) package combines Orchestrion-JS AST transformation with a Cordis-owned patch registry and runtime bridge. A trusted plugin registers a patch (target + operation + handler) against another plugin's module, file, and function; the transform hooks rewrite the target function's body to publish a call record to a process-local bridge channel, and the runtime dispatches it to the currently registered handler. When no handler is active (disabled, disposed, or never enabled), transformed code delegates to the original body untouched.
+
+[`@apm-js-collab/code-transformer`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@apm-js-collab/code-transformer) is the transformation engine, consumed from npm (not vendored) and recorded in the third-party notice. [`@apm-js-collab/tracing-hooks`](https://github.com/apm-js-collab/tracing-hooks) informed the load-time integration design but is not a dependency: the package implements its own synchronous `module.registerHooks` + CJS `_compile` hooks so the bridge channel, not a tracing channel, carries the dispatch.
+
+### Scope and package placement
+
+The package lives at `packages/self-modification/cordis-fabric/`, is not part of the default composition, and makes no stability or compatibility promise. It owns:
+
+- The patch descriptor model in `src/types.ts`.
+- The Cordis `FabricService` (`src/service.ts`) that registers and disposes trusted handlers as fiber effects.
+- The process-local runtime registry (`src/runtime.ts`) that dispatches bridge calls without exposing `Context` to target modules.
+- The bridge (`src/bridge.ts`): a `globalThis`-installed handle that transformed code publishes to, with a synchronous exchange slot for the dispatch result.
+- The Orchestrion custom transform (`src/transform.ts`) that rewrites matched functions for `before`/`around`/`after`/`replace`.
+- The Node bootstrap (`src/node-loader.ts`) that installs transformation hooks before target imports.
+- Invariant, runtime, and child-process load-time tests under `tests/`.
+
+The package does not modify `vendor/cordis`, does not make `ctx.intercept()` a general function interceptor, and does not use Cordis Loader entry order as an implicit patch barrier.
+
+### Patch contract
+
+| Field | Contract |
+|---|---|
+| `id` | Globally unique patch identity within one Fabric runtime; duplicate registration updates the existing claim rather than creating a second claim. Must match `[A-Za-z0-9._:/+-]{1,120}`. |
+| `target` | Module name, semver range, file path, and one function query or `astQuery` selector. |
+| `operation` | `before`, `around`, `after`, or `replace`, with sync and Promise semantics. |
+| `priority` | Numeric ordering key; higher priorities run first, equal priorities preserve stable installation order. |
+| `handler` | Trusted imported code; executable handlers are never deserialized from YAML or model input. |
+
+Behavior semantics:
+
+- `before` mutates the call arguments in place before the original body runs.
+- `around` decides whether the original body runs and may replace its result; skipping `invoke()` vetoes the body.
+- `after` observes or replaces the successful result; async targets are rewritten after promise settlement.
+- `replace` owns the call; the original body only runs if the handler calls `invoke()`.
+- Malformed patch fields fail during validation; a well-formed target that does not match the installed version or file layout remains untransformed. Transform and load failures are reported as structured errors.
+- Multiple patches on one function stack through the shared bridge, ordered by priority: instrumentations apply in ascending priority order, so a higher-priority handler runs first (the outermost layer), and equal priorities keep installation order. An exclusive conflict between `replace` patches on the same target is rejected at registration.
+
+The transform keeps the matched function's signature, `.length`, and `this` binding. The original body moves into a `traced` closure that replays it via `apply(this, args)` over the reconstructed arguments array, so `before` mutations reach the body. Matched nodes include function declarations, function expressions, methods, and arrows with plain identifier parameters; expression-bodied arrows receive a synthesized block body. Generator targets and arrows whose bodies read the enclosing `arguments` object are skipped because the traced function would change their semantics.
+
+### Cordis lifecycle and runtime bridge
+
+`FabricService` provides `register`, `list`, `disable`, and `enable`. Each registration is owned by the current fiber through `ctx.effect()`; disposing the fiber disables and removes the patch. The transformed function publishes to the bridge keyed by patch id; the bridge is Cordis-free because target code may be evaluated before any Cordis fiber exists and may be shared by Host and browser module systems.
+
+Patch ownership is process-wide while the owning fiber is active; there is no automatic per-request or per-child-context scoping. Code transformation and runtime handler disposal are separate operations: an already-evaluated module keeps its transformed code, while disabling a patch makes transformed code delegate to the original body.
+
+### Node Host integration
+
+The Node path requires an explicit pre-Cordis bootstrap:
+
+1. `installFabricHooks([patchInstrumentation(patch), ...])` installs the bridge handle and the transformation hooks before any target module is imported.
+2. The hooks match target modules by package, version, and file through `module-details-from-path` and Orchestrion's matcher, falling back to the nearest `package.json` (`nodePackageResolver`) when the npm-layout parser cannot name the module — Node realpaths workspace links, so a workspace package's loaded URL has no `node_modules` boundary, and the nearest manifest is what lets a patch target a first-party package at its real path.
+3. The transform rewrites matching functions; ESM files are handled by the sync `load` hook, CommonJS files by the `_compile` patch, with a shared seen-set guarding the double path.
+4. `ctx.plugin(FabricService)` mounts the registry, and patch plugins register handlers through `ctx.fabric.register`.
+
+The hooks use Node's public `module.registerHooks` (sync hooks, Node ≥ 22.22.3 / ≥ 24.11.1). `registerHooks` has no unregister: hooks compose and stay for the process lifetime, so the returned disposer deactivates the installation's state (hooks become pass-through, cached transformers are freed) rather than removing the hook functions. Each installation captures its own state, so concurrent installations transform through independent matchers and the shared CommonJS `_compile` wrapper chains every active installation in installation order (the same stacking the sync hook chain produces). The vendored Loader's private `ModuleLoader` object is not used as a transformation API.
+
+`registerHooks` exists from 22.19.0, but before 22.22.3 / 24.11.1 its synchronous load chain returns no source for CommonJS modules when loader-thread hooks (`module.register`, e.g. tsx on those versions) are also present, which crashes Node's load validation; the loader therefore gates the sync path on the stable versions and falls back to `module.register` below them, keeping every hook on one loader-thread chain. The gate mirrors the engine range's two families (22.x and ≥ 24) instead of trusting the function's presence.
+
+### Configuration, trust, and authority
+
+Patch handlers are trusted code bound at registration time; YAML and model-written values may select a target and ordinary data but may not carry executable transform functions. `cordis_mount` temporary plugins and repository plugins must not receive Fabric capability without an explicit grant, because a patch can alter unrelated code in the host process. Diagnostics report patch id, target package, version, file, selector, and failure reason without dumping source or secrets.
+
+## Testing
+
+`tests/runtime.spec.ts` covers registry lifecycle, ordering, duplicate ids, re-registration ownership, id validation, service validation errors, and bridge listener semantics. `tests/browser-transform.spec.ts`, `tests/watched-transform.spec.ts`, and `tests/browser-runtime.spec.ts` cover the browser transform, the file-backed watched transform, TypeScript/JSX preparation, browser bridge, service mounting, and fiber disposal. `tests/load-time.spec.ts`, `tests/multi-install.spec.ts`, and `tests/async-fallback.spec.ts` run fresh child-process cases against fixture packages under `tests/fixtures/node_modules/`, covering Node sync and async hooks, CommonJS, concurrent installations, disposal isolation, and a workspace-shaped fixture reached at its real path (no `node_modules` boundary) so non-removable hooks and transformed-module caches do not leak between cases. `apps/cli/tests/fabric-bootstrap.spec.ts` covers launcher composition and malformed static configuration.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+**Cordis events and waterfall only.** This remains the preferred seam when B exposes a cooperative event, but it cannot reach a private function or first-import behavior in a plugin without such an event.
+
+**`monkey-around` or direct object monkey patching.** This is useful for an already exposed service method and may complement Fabric, but it cannot reach module-private closures, cannot guarantee first-import coverage, and does not provide source-level target diagnostics.
+
+**`import-in-the-middle` alone.** It can modify existing ESM exports before or during loading, but it cannot express arbitrary AST injection or reach unexported implementation details. It remains a possible Node export-level fallback, not the primary engine.
+
+**OpenTelemetry Instrumentation.** Its module version matching and wrap/unpatch lifecycle are useful prior art, but its public contract is telemetry-oriented and would add unnecessary dependency and semantic weight to a general behavior-patching facility.
+
+**Modifying vendored Cordis or Loader internals.** This would couple the feature to unstable Node and Loader implementation details, expand the vendored local-modification surface, and make upstream synchronization harder. The implementation uses a separate bootstrap and the public module-hook boundary.
+
+**Placement in stable `packages/cordis`.** The package lives in `packages/cordis/` because its Node composition, security authority, disposal semantics, and browser runtime wiring form one Cordis integration; it remains opt-in and outside the default composition.
+
+## Consequences
+
+AST-level patches are coupled to module versions, file paths, compiler output, and syntax shape; selectors and version ranges reduce accidental matches but cannot make an upstream implementation stable.
+
+Transformation happens before module evaluation, while Cordis disposal happens after evaluation; handler removal is reversible at runtime, and an already-evaluated module re-transforms only through the explicit `retransformCommonJs`/`retransformEsm` invalidation APIs. Both drop the module's cache entries (the ESM one evicts the Node-internal `loadCache` entry, the same mechanism the vendored Loader's HMR uses; the CommonJS one clears `require.cache` **and** the same file's `loadCache` entry, so both graphs observe the fresh evaluation), and a failed ESM re-import restores the evicted entry so the previous instance survives (the vendored HMR's rollback). This is the explicit trade-off for first-import coverage.
+
+A patch has process-level code authority inside the target module and can bypass ordinary extension seams. Trust checks, explicit opt-in, and the default denial for model-written temporary plugins are mandatory rather than optional hardening.
+
+## Deferred
+
+- **Browser-runtime re-transformation without a rebuild.** The browser's native module map has no eviction API equivalent to Node's `loadCache`, so an already-materialized client module cannot be re-transformed in place; changing the transform set always goes through a bundle rebuild (see the build trigger under Resolved). In-place invalidation APIs (`retransformEsm`/`retransformCommonJs`) stay Node-host features.
+
+## Resolved
+
+- **Browser build trigger for patch-set changes** — `createWatchedBrowserTransform(patchesPath, resolve)` builds the bundler transform from a JSON patches file (static stubs, the same shape the launcher row's `config.patches` carries; `filePath` strings only), registers the file in the bundler's watch graph on every module through the `clientBundle` source-transform plugin's forwarded `addWatchFile`, and rebuilds the underlying matcher only when the file content changes (the hook-entry's read-per-use, rebuild-on-change pattern). Under `tsdown --watch` (`scripts/dev-web.ts`) a patch edit rebuilds the bundle, and the client-hmr chain (stat poll → `rebuilt` frame → invalidate/prefetch/fiber swap over the web module table's own evictable `loadCache`) delivers the new transform set to the browser — closing the gap the Deferred entry above defers only in its no-rebuild form. Malformed files fail the build loudly. Covered by `tests/watched-transform.spec.ts`, `scripts/client-bundle-source-transform.spec.ts` (the preset's `addWatchFile` forwarding), and `scripts/dev-web-fabric.spec.ts` (the watch-mode rebuild end to end).
+- **Browser/Web runtime wiring** — the package moved to `packages/cordis/`; its client half (`./client`, a dshClient row disabled by default in the web roster) installs the bridge and mounts `ctx.fabric` in the browser Cordis tree. Client bundles transform at build time and fall back to the original body until that entry materializes. The upstream `createCodeTransformer` adapter is not used: it resolves module identity through `module-details-from-path` (a `node_modules` boundary), while client bundles build from repository source paths; the package's `repoSourceResolver` covers that layout.
+- **Async `module.register` fallback** — Node versions without a reliable `registerHooks` (including 22.19–22.22.2 / 24.0–24.11.0, where the sync chain crashes on CommonJS alongside loader-thread hooks) load the `./hook-entry` loader-thread module (reusing the browser transform with the node-modules resolver), with the CJS `_compile` patch handling CommonJS. The entry is registered exactly once and reads a shared configuration file — rewritten by the main thread on every installation and disposal — on each load, so the loader-thread transform always reflects the current installation stack: `retransformEsm`, disposal, and concurrent installations behave as on the sync path. Each active installation gets its own transform and the source chains through them in installation order, so cross-installation nesting matches the sync hook chain and the CJS `_compile` wrapper (the chain is rebuilt only when the configuration content changes). The pid-scoped file is removed on process exit.
+- **Multi-match selectors rewrite every match** — the upstream transformer's `index` filter defaults to the first match only, which silently under-patched a selector picking several functions in one file (e.g. two classes defining the same method). Fabric flips the default to `index: null` (every match), lets name queries carry `functionQuery.index` and raw `astQuery` targets carry `target.index` (validated as a non-negative integer or null) to select a single match, and rejects constructor targets loudly at transformation time: a moved constructor body cannot carry `super()` (a syntax error at evaluation) or `new.target` (silently undefined), so the transform fails with a clear error naming a method-or-factory alternative instead of emitting an unevaluatable module.
+- **Exclusive `replace` conflict detection** — a second `replace` patch on the same target (module + version + file + selector) fails at registration.
+- **Arrow and generator function targets** — arrows transform via an argument array rebuilt from their parameter patterns (lexical `this`); identifiers, rest elements, defaults, and destructuring all work because the patterns bind their names before the injected statements run; expression-bodied arrows get a synthesized block body; a body referencing the enclosing `arguments` object is preserved by capturing it first, and only a parameter literally named `arguments` (which would shadow that capture) is skipped. Generators delegate through `yield*`: the traced generator preserves iteration on the no-handler and delegated paths, a non-iterable handler replacement is returned directly, and `after` observes the generator object before iteration.
+- **Web TypeScript sources** — `.ts`/`.tsx` sources are stripped to plain JavaScript before transformation in the browser build path.
+- **DSH launcher bootstrap** — `installFabricBootstrap` (in `apps/cli/src/profile-boot.ts`) runs in `boot()`'s `prepare` phase and installs the transformation hooks when the composed rows carry a `cordis-fabric` row with `config.patches` (static descriptors; handlers stay trusted code); the row's `disabled` flag governs plugin mounting, not the load-time bootstrap; consumers can still call `bootstrapFabric` explicitly.
