+# Agent Note: Keyed-slot arbitration facade for Fabric Client API
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-08-08-slot-arbitration-facade.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+Keyed-slot exclusivity is a deliberate host invariant: `dsh-client-ui-slots` throws when a duplicate key is registered, so exactly one plugin renders a UI space. The first real consumer, ui-bash, had to inspect `ctx.slots.entries(...)` before registering and fall back to the built-in sample when another plugin owned the key.
+
+That hand-rolled pattern gives consumers no declaration of priority, no automatic takeover when an owner is disposed, and no negotiated degradation for a losing plugin. Fabric patches cannot safely fork `slots.register` without duplicating ui-slots internals, so arbitration belongs in the cooperative `fabricClient` facade.
+
+## Decision
+
+`ctx.fabricClient.registerKeyedSlot(options, component)` adds an arbitrated registration face in `packages/self-modification/cordis-fabric-api/src/client/` while `registerSlot` keeps its exact passthrough behavior and direct `ctx.slots.register` calls retain the host conflict.
+
+### Claim model
+
+The facade keeps a claim table per slot name and key with the current owner and waiting claimants. `options.priority` selects the highest-priority claimant; equal priorities preserve registration order and emit a warning naming both plugins.
+
+The winner registers through `ctx.slots.register`, while a loser registers nothing and receives a `SlotClaim`. An owner claim disposes its registration and promotes the next waiting claimant; a waiting claim remains queued until it is disposed or promoted, and optional `onGain` runs on promotion.
+
+Optional `onLost?: (winner: { plugin?: string }) => void` tells a displaced claimant that another claimant won or took over, allowing it to deliberately degrade its UI instead of disappearing.
+
+### Takeover semantics
+
+A later higher-priority claimant becomes the logical owner immediately, but it does not force-dispose the incumbent; the incumbent remains mounted until it disposes itself, avoiding an abrupt unmount during an interaction. The live `SlotClaim.owner` getter reflects the current ownership state.
+
+### Scope boundary
+
+Only `packages/self-modification/cordis-fabric-api` changes: the client module gains the claim table, arbitrated method, `SlotClaim` type, and tests. `dsh-client-ui-slots` remains unchanged, and the claim table belongs to the FabricClientService instance and is cleaned up with its fiber effects.
+
+## Testing
+
+- Two claimants for one key verify priority ownership, queued `owner: false`, promotion after owner disposal, and `onGain`.
+- Equal priorities verify registration order and the warning naming both plugins.
+- Higher-priority takeover verifies `onLost` and confirms that the incumbent registration is not force-disposed.
+- Direct `ctx.slots.register` still throws for a taken key, and `registerSlot` remains a behavior-preserving passthrough.
+- A Loader-backed composition verifies the rendered owner for the ui-bash sample-competition shape.
+- The Fabric API and ui-bash suites, host/client typechecks, oxlint, and knip remain the verification targets.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+- **Change ui-slots to multi-owner rendering.** Rejected because one renderer per UI space is the host design and renderer-side merging has no declared per-slot semantics.
+- **Patch `slots.register` with Fabric `replace`.** Rejected because a fork would duplicate host internals, drift with ui-slots, and affect every direct registration.
+- **Document the hand-rolled pattern.** Rejected because it leaves first-come-first-served ownership, no priority, no takeover, and no negotiated degradation.
+- **Put arbitration in compat.** Rejected because slots are a browser-side concern and `fabricClient` is the cooperative web facade that already delegates to `ctx.slots`.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Existing host registration semantics and direct conflict errors remain unchanged; only consumers opting into the facade receive arbitration.
+- Higher-priority takeover can briefly leave two rows mounted, but `onLost` gives the incumbent an explicit path to dispose itself instead of forcing an abrupt unmount.
+- A priority value declares intent rather than guaranteeing ownership; equal or tied highest priorities still resolve by registration order with a warning.
+- The claim table adds one method and one claim type to the narrow client face and is lifecycle-scoped to the service instance.
