+# Agent Note: Fabric HMR end-to-end proof
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-08-12-fabric-hmr-e2e-proof.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+Fabric's hot-reload behavior was only proven component-wise: the runtime
+transfers patch ownership across same-owner re-registrations, the load hooks
+re-transform already-evaluated modules, the browser transform rebuilds its
+matcher from a watched patches file, and the app-boot user-patch layer
+refreshes transactionally. Nothing chained those pieces through a real
+Loader tree, so the web bundle still disables the shared module-reload HMR
+row with `TODO: Re-enable shared HMR for Web after its reload lifecycle is
+tested` — there was no automated proof that an HMR refresh keeps a Fabric
+transformation live and correctly owned.
+
+## Decision
+
+`cordis-fabric` gains an end-to-end suite (`tests/hmr-e2e.spec.ts` +
+`tests/hmr-e2e-runner.mjs`) that boots a real Loader tree — vendored
+`cordis-plugin-loader`, `-include`, `-hmr`, `-timer` plus the Fabric service
+and transformation hooks — and drives both HMR surfaces a deployment can
+use, against the transformed target fixture:
+
+- **Config-only HMR (row lifecycle).** The HMR plugin's `registerConfig`
+  observes a user patch layer (`cordis.patch.json`), the same contract the
+  app's `watchUserPatches` uses. Editing the consumer row's `disabled` flag
+  must unmount the consumer plugin and release its Fabric patch (transformed
+  `add(2,3)` falls back to the original `5`), and re-enabling must remount
+  and re-register it (`23` again). Two full cycles prove the lifecycle is
+  repeatable without residue.
+- **Module-reload HMR (plugin regeneration).** The consumer plugin file
+  lives inside the HMR watch root; rewriting it triggers the HMR plugin's
+  partial reload, which re-imports the module and re-applies the row under
+  the same loader entry. `fiber.entry` survives, so the re-registration is a
+  same-owner take-over: the new generation's handler runs (`23` → `203`) and
+  the stale fiber's disposer does not unregister it (a second call stays
+  `203`).
+
+Each case runs in a fresh child process: the synchronous module hooks cannot
+be unregistered and the already-transformed module cache must not leak
+between cases. The runner resolves `cordis-fabric/src/*` through tsx, so the
+suite passes on a clean tree.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+- **Place the config-only case under `app-boot` and call
+  `watchUserPatches`.** Rejected: `cordis-fabric` is a pure Cordis package
+  and must not depend on a DSH package even in devDependencies.
+  `registerConfig` is the exact API `watchUserPatches` wraps, so the test
+  exercises the same contract without the DSH layer.
+- **A runnable demo command instead of tests.** Rejected: a demo is not
+  repeatable evidence and cannot enter CI; the spec's child runners double
+  as the demo (each prints `PASS`/`FAIL` lines with the observed values).
+
+## Consequences
+
+Both HMR surfaces now have automated end-to-end proof that a refresh keeps
+Fabric transformations live, correctly registered, and correctly released.
+The evidence unblocks the decision on re-enabling the web bundle's shared
+`hmr` row (the enablement itself stays a separate choice). Chokidar
+coalesces rapid writes to one file, so consecutive patch-file writes must
+wait out the atomic-write window (75 ms, matching the app-boot user-patch
+tests) or a refresh is silently dropped — the runner documents this
+directly at its `settleWatch` helper.
